    "author": "Simon Armitage",
    "rights": "protected_summary_only",
    "cards": [
      [
        "A conversational account contains unresolved threat",
        "A first-person soldier recounts a group’s shooting of a man during an operation against looters. The account leaves uncertainty about whether the man carried a weapon. Analyse that uncertainty as part of the voice’s continuing difficulty; do not silently convert it into certainty in either direction. The speaker is a constructed voice, not automatically Armitage confessing to an action."
      ],
      [
        "A shared action becomes a personal burden",
        "Early collective references distribute participation among several soldiers. As the account develops, the first-person experience of remembering becomes more prominent. The killing’s physical aftermath and the speaker’s repeated encounters with its trace resist a quick narrative closure. The poem asks the reader to consider how an event can remain present after it is apparently over."
      ],
      [
        "Leaving the place does not leave the memory",
        "The speaker’s return home on leave does not end the mental recurrence. Attempts to suppress the experience fail, and the ending makes responsibility feel immediate and personal. A literary account can represent distress and guilt without providing enough information for a clinical diagnosis or proving how every veteran responds. Attend to the specific voice and its unresolved question."
      ]
    ],
